# coreutilsh
◆ arch : Hardware name (same as uname -m). To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ basename : Strip directory and suffix from filenames. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ dirname : Strip last component from file name. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ echo : Line of text. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ env : Run a program in a modified environment. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ false : Do nothing, unsuccessfully. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ hostname : Show or set system host name. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ locale : Show or set system host name. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ logname : Print user's login name. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ nproc : Print the number of processing units available. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ printenv : Print all or part of environment. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ printf : Print data. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ pwd : Print name of current/working directory. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ test : Check file types and compare values. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ touch : Change file timestamps. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ true : Do nothing, successfully. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ uname : Print system information. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ which : A utility to show the full path of commands. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ whoami : Print effective user name. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.
◆ yes : Output a string repeatedly until killed. To install it use the --coreutilsh flag or the .coreutilsh extension.

